# Security Standards (DevSecOps)

## Shift-Left Security Principles
Security checks MUST occur at the earliest possible point:
| Stage | Security Control |
|-------|------------------|
| Local development | Pre-commit secret scanning (Gitleaks) |
| Pull Request | SAST scan, dependency vulnerability check |
| Build | Container image scanning |
| Deploy | DAST scan against staging |
| Runtime | Anomaly detection, audit logging |

## OWASP Top 10 (2021+) — Critical Items
All developers MUST understand and design against:
- A01: Broken Access Control — Enforce authorization at every layer; never trust client-supplied roles.
- A02: Cryptographic Failures — Never implement cryptography yourself; use vetted libraries. TLS everywhere.
- A03: Injection — Parameterized queries always. Never string-concatenate SQL, shell commands, or LDAP queries.
- A05: Security Misconfiguration — Default credentials MUST be changed; debug endpoints MUST be disabled in production.
- A06: Vulnerable Components — Automated dependency scanning in every CI pipeline.
- A07: Auth Failures — Use established auth libraries; never implement JWT validation manually.

## Secrets Management Rules
- Secrets MUST NOT be hardcoded anywhere — not in code, configs, comments, or documentation.
- `.env` files are acceptable for local development ONLY and MUST be in `.gitignore`.
- Production secrets MUST come from: HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, or equivalent.
- All service-to-service authentication MUST use short-lived tokens, not long-lived API keys.

## Dependency Vulnerability Policy
- Dependency scanning MUST run on every CI pipeline.
- CRITICAL severity vulnerabilities MUST block deployment.
- HIGH severity vulnerabilities MUST be resolved within 7 days.
- MEDIUM severity vulnerabilities MUST be tracked and resolved within 30 days.

## Sensitive Data Prohibition (Logging)
The following MUST NEVER appear in logs under any circumstances:
- Passwords and authentication credentials
- Credit card numbers, bank account numbers
- Social Security Numbers, national IDs
- Personal Health Information (PHI)
- API keys, tokens, secrets
- Full street addresses of private individuals

Where partial data is required for debugging, use masking:
```json
{
  "card_number": "****-****-****-4242",
  "email": "j***@example.com"
}
```

## Pre-Commit Secret Scanning
Pre-commit hooks with Gitleaks or TruffleHog are REQUIRED.
Repositories MUST be scanned on every CI run.

## Incident Response
If secrets are accidentally committed:
1. Force-push to remove from history
2. Rotate ALL exposed credentials immediately
3. Audit access logs for unauthorized access

## Gold Standard Examples
- See `examples/` directory for secure coding patterns in each language

## What NOT to Do
- Never hardcode secrets in code or config files
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, card numbers)
- Never commit `.env` files to version control
- Never use long-lived API keys for service-to-service auth
- Never skip dependency vulnerability scans in CI
